#include (../Settings/defs.ves)

In the basis analysis, one is taught that if $X$ is a metric space and $A$ is a
subset of $X$ then $x\in\overline{A}$ if and only if there is a sequence
$(x_n)_n$ in $A$ that converges to $x$. However, such fact does not hold for
general topological spaces. Actually, several properties of sequences in metric
spaces break when we generalize those in the arbitrary topological spaces. This
makes a reason introducing an object which acts like a sequence more general
sense. There are two things that generalize sequences: nets, and filters.
In here, we are going to study about nets. Filter theory is introduced in the
appendix.

First, we need to see what are nets. Note that nets are defined even if $X$ has
no topology.
useenv defin [Nets] {
    Let $X$ be a nonempty set. A {\itshape directed set}\index{directed set} is
    a nonempty set $A$ equipped with a preorder $<=$ (reflexive and transitive
    relation) satisfying the following condition:
    #enum ((\roman*)) {
        \item For $\alpha,\beta\in A$, there exists $\gamma\in A$ such that
        $\alpha <= \gamma$ and $\beta <= \gamma$.
    }
    A {\itshape net}\index{net} in $X$ is a function $x:A->X$ from a directed
    set $A$ to $X$. We usually write $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ or simply
    $(x_\alpha)$.
}

Below definition will be used to define a convergence of net later.
useenv defin [Eventually and Frequently] {
    We say that a net $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ is {\itshape
    eventually}\index{net!eventually in} in $E\subset X$ if there exists
    $\alpha_0\in A$ such that $x_\alpha\in E$ for all $\alpha >= \alpha_0$.

    A net $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ is \it{frequently}\index{net!frequently in}
    in $E\subset X$ if for every $\alpha\in A$ there exists $\beta >= \alpha$
    such that $x_\beta\in E$.
}

useenv remark {
    Every sequence is a net because clearly $\N$ is a directed set with an usual
    ordering. From the definition, the term \lq\lq eventually\rq\rq\ implies
    \lq\lq frequently\rq\rq\ but not the converse. For instance, consider a net
    (actually a sequence) $((-1)^n)_{n\in\N}$.
    This net does not eventually in the interval $(0,2)$ and this fact is easily
    obtained from the definition. However, for every $n$, take any even number
    $m$ greater than $n$. In this case, since $(-1)^m = 1 \in (0,2)$, given net
    is frequently in $(0,2)$.
}

Since nets is a generalization of sequences, it is natual to consider what is
the analogous definition of subsequences for nets. The definition of which is
the following.

useenv defin [Subnet] {
    Let $X$ be a nonempty set. A net $(y_\beta)_{\beta\in B}$ is a
    \it{subnet}\index{net!subnet,subnet} of a net $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ if
    there exists a function $h : B -> A$ such that
    #enum ((\roman*)) {
        \item For every $\alpha\in A$, there exists $\beta_0\in B$ such that
        $h(\beta) >= \alpha$ for all $\beta >= \beta_0$.
        \item $y_\beta = x_{h(\beta)}$.
    }
}

useenv remark {
    Clearly every net is a subnet of itself.
    Unlike sequences, the cardinality of $B$ might be larger than $A$.
}

There is an important relation between terminologies \lq\lq eventually\rq\rq\
and \lq\lq frequently\rq\rq.

#label(prop:eventually-frequently)
useenv prop {
    Let $X$ be a nonempty set, $(x_\alpha)_{\alpha\in A}$ be a net which is
    frequently in some subset $E\subset X$.
    Then there exists a subnet which is eventually in $E$.
    \proof
}
